WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.004
[Description] Justin sits facing the camera in a dark T-shirt against a plain wall, hands

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:07.658
clasped, a tattoo sleeve on his left forearm.

00:00:08.228 --> 00:00:11.267
My name is Justin. I work for Perspective Tester, and

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:13.907
I'm blind or low vision, and I'm a native assistive

00:00:13.907 --> 00:00:18.747
technology user. My vision presents with no central vision and

00:00:18.747 --> 00:00:23.268
limited peripheral vision. Without assistive technology, I certainly don't think

00:00:23.268 --> 00:00:24.747
I would be where I am today.

00:00:24.788 --> 00:00:26.828
You know, I can't see the screen when it's back

00:00:26.828 --> 00:00:27.988
this far. I see a little bit of light and

00:00:27.988 --> 00:00:31.713
[Description] Over Justin's shoulder: a large monitor shows the Google home page, and he gestures

00:00:31.713 --> 00:00:35.437
at it from his chair. Then the screen magnifies until the “G” of the

00:00:35.437 --> 00:00:39.162
Google logo and the mouse pointer fill the display.

00:00:41.082 --> 00:00:43.962
contrast in certain places, but I really need to get

00:00:43.962 --> 00:00:47.121
down on the individual letter size to see what things

00:00:47.121 --> 00:00:50.242
are. I use screen readers and zoom and all the

00:00:50.242 --> 00:00:53.202
different tools to help me navigate life and to be

00:00:53.202 --> 00:00:56.082
as independent as I possibly can. And so when it

00:00:56.082 --> 00:00:59.161
doesn't work, it's extremely frustrating. It's pretty limiting, but I

00:00:59.161 --> 00:01:00.882
can still function and get around.

00:01:01.622 --> 00:01:04.619
[Description] Justin leans in close to the monitor. At this magnification only part of the

00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:07.616
Google logo fits on screen; he moves the pointer to the search box and

00:01:07.616 --> 00:01:10.614
types.

00:01:11.113 --> 00:01:13.354
With nothing in the middle, I can't see my screens

00:01:13.354 --> 00:01:17.194
very well. I use assistive technology in a myriad of

00:01:17.194 --> 00:01:20.834
different ways. When I'm using my computer, I'm zooming down

00:01:20.834 --> 00:01:24.634
to individual letters and navigating my screens that way.

00:01:24.674 --> 00:01:28.214
Sometimes it's really difficult to read long paragraphs of text

00:01:28.214 --> 00:01:32.078
[Description] Justin pans across a magnified website a few letters at a time, the text

00:01:32.078 --> 00:01:35.941
blurring as it moves: “Sign in”, a yellow “Join now” button and a black

00:01:35.941 --> 00:01:39.805
banner with “Get tickets” in yellow. Zoomed out, the page shows a notification popup

00:01:39.805 --> 00:01:43.668
with “Later” and “Subscribe” buttons.

00:01:44.608 --> 00:01:47.568
with one letter on the screen at a time, and

00:01:47.568 --> 00:01:50.647
so I use VoiceOver at a pretty fast speaking rate.

00:01:50.647 --> 00:01:53.907
It's really important that a lot of websites and mobile

00:01:53.907 --> 00:01:56.168
apps work well with screen readers, to have it read

00:01:56.168 --> 00:01:58.608
it to me.

00:01:59.208 --> 00:02:03.607
[Description] At normal size, Justin scrolls through the website's menu and articles. Then he zooms

00:02:03.607 --> 00:02:08.005
in on a blue heading beginning “Our F”, with the text cursor over it.

00:02:08.605 --> 00:02:11.805
It's really amazing, but when it doesn't work, it creates

00:02:11.805 --> 00:02:13.484
huge limiting factors.

00:02:13.725 --> 00:02:16.045
My name's Justin, and this is my perspective.

